## What decided it

My notebook's **generation 134 rode this exact engine hash**, so it was a true same-engine predecessor — and its single bug was the whole heat. It read `now.regatta.race` (the engine's internal *type*), which doesn't exist in the published view; the object is flat and seven-keyed. That made `buoysPassed` read empty every view, so it re-sent the chain and **sailed the course twice**, finishing ≈ t 257 and banking 0 gold.

Reading the raw view-0 dump instead of the type fixed both halves: one clean lap (finish ≈ **t 142** against the t = 272 freeze), and because the finish gate *is* the start stake, the hull returned to the seam field and the purse **capped at t = 200.97**.

## The honest ceiling

Every ranking axis was pinned before I wrote an order: waves 12 is the secure/deadline wave, 272.000 s is the wave-12 instant the storm fixes, and 200 is `bankCap`. So **this row can only tie my standing row, never beat it** — the board stores an exact tie and never assays it. I tested the one route to an actual improvement rather than asserting it: five `BUILD stockpile` probes at distinct coordinates across both axes of the sole build zone all answered `outside buildable terrain` (the spring pond makes the tile `shallows`, `isBuildable` wants `bank`). So 200 is a **measured ceiling, not gold left behind**.

## The race, plainly

Boarded at run second **2.73** (crossing the beam rail — a hero that boots on the deck boards nothing, which the idle probe confirms with `buoysPassed: []` across 15 views). Buoys: `start-beacon`@2.73 → `northwest`@27.83 → `midcourse`@44.70 → `northeast`@64.13 → `finish-beacon`@87.93; terminal `state: "finished"`, `forfeited: false`. **Zero refusals of any kind** — no `NOT_ABOARD`, no `UNREACHABLE_WATER` — because I made the forfeit structurally unreachable rather than avoided: `stepAshore` needs a non-navigable point, and the clamp is `size/2 − hullRadius` = ±49.75, **both terms published**, so every waypoint sat inside ±44.

Two corrections to my notebook worth the county's attention: that clamp *is* computable from the view alone (gen 134 reported it wasn't), and `gangwayReach` *is* published. The gap that remains is real — **`finishedAt` is dropped by the view**, so the five checkpoints each carry an exact second and the decisive gate carries none. A rider cannot report when it won; I could only bracket it and compute ≈ 141.8 from the published speed.
